You are working on the Alva game repository:

hiyroscript/alva

Use this repository as the implementation target.

Use the Seren repository as the visual reference:

hiyroscript/seren

The goal is to redesign Alva’s color system across the UI so it has the same general visual discipline and atmosphere as Seren—black/charcoal dominant, gray surfaces, off-white typography, restrained borders, and one strong accent color—but replace Seren’s red accent with green.

There is one extremely important scope rule:

Only the Alva Home Screen may receive a structural/layout redesign.

Every other screen must retain its current layout, DOM structure, spacing system, controls, functionality, navigation, information hierarchy, and gameplay behavior. Other screens should only inherit the new dark/green visual palette through shared styling and narrowly targeted color changes.

Do not redesign Mode Select, Character Select, Map Select, Help/Credits, Battle, pause/results UI, overlays, or any other screen.

Reference direction

Seren establishes its visual identity using a very small palette:

--void: #050506;
--ground: #0A0B0C;
--asphalt: #141518;
--ink: #F4F5F6;
--ink-2: #A7ACB3;
--ink-3: #71767E;
--ink-4: #4C5158;
--line: rgba(255,255,255,.09);
--line-2: rgba(255,255,255,.16);
--line-3: rgba(255,255,255,.28);
--wash: rgba(255,255,255,.04);
--wash-2: rgba(255,255,255,.07);

Seren then uses red as its sole interface accent.

For Alva, use the same philosophy but make green the accent.

A suitable Alva accent family is:

--accent: #2FBF63;
--accent-hi: #7CF7A6;
--accent-lo: #1F8F4A;
--accent-wash: rgba(47,191,99,.14);

Do not introduce blue, purple, orange, yellow, cyan, or additional decorative hues.

The finished UI should visually read as:

near-black → charcoal → gray → off-white → green accent

Green should be deliberate and relatively scarce. The game should remain primarily black/gray/white rather than becoming a “green UI.”

Global Alva color conversion

Alva currently defines a white-dominant monochrome system at the top of styles.css. Convert that system into a dark Seren-like system.

Target approximately:

--bg: #050506;
--surface: #0A0B0C;
--surface-subtle: #141518;
--surface-muted: #1C1F24;
--surface-sunken: #08090A;
--text: #F4F5F6;
--text-strong: #FFFFFF;
--text-secondary: #A7ACB3;
--text-muted: #71767E;
--text-faint: #4C5158;
--border: rgba(255,255,255,.09);
--border-strong: rgba(255,255,255,.16);
--border-hover: rgba(255,255,255,.28);
--black: #050506;
--white: #FFFFFF;
--accent: #2FBF63;
--accent-hi: #7CF7A6;
--accent-lo: #1F8F4A;
--accent-wash: rgba(47,191,99,.14);

Do not blindly invert existing variables if doing so creates incorrect semantics. Alva currently uses variables such as --ink, --black, and --white for both text and filled controls. Refactor individual rules where necessary so that dark surfaces remain dark, text remains light, and green is used for intentional emphasis.

Primary interactive emphasis should generally become green, similar to how Seren uses red.

Examples include primary actions, active/focused selection accents, small progress/indicator accents, and selected-state borders where appropriate.

Secondary controls should generally remain transparent or charcoal with thin gray borders and off-white/gray text.

Hover states should lighten surfaces slightly rather than introducing new colors.

Pressed green actions can use --accent-lo.

Green hover/highlight treatment can use --accent-hi sparingly.

Subtle selected backgrounds may use --accent-wash.

Do not make every border green.

Do not make every heading green.

Do not tint entire cards green.

Do not use neon glow everywhere.

The green needs to work like Seren’s red: a controlled visual signal.

