Home Screen — full redesign allowed

The Home Screen is the exception.

Rework the Alva Home Screen so its composition feels strongly related to Seren’s Home Screen while still clearly being Alva.

The existing Alva Home currently consists of a header, text/action column, and a contained character preview card.

Replace the existing character-preview concept.

The new Home composition should have a large editorial wordmark/text area on the left and an abstract moving road occupying the right side of the screen.

The road replaces the current character placeholder/preview panel.

Do not place a fighter sprite on the Home Screen.

Home visual hierarchy

The background should be full-screen near-black/charcoal.

Make the existing ALVA geometric SVG wordmark the dominant visual element.

Do not replace Alva’s existing logo with a generic font.

Continue using logoSVG() from:

js/ui/logo.js

The wordmark should become dramatically larger than it is currently—closer to Seren’s oversized “SEREN” Home title.

Think editorial title scale rather than application-logo scale.

Keep it off-white/white.

Above the title, retain a small mono uppercase kicker such as the existing:

2D sprite fighting game

This should be muted gray.

Below or close to the title, keep a short supporting line if it contributes to balance, but do not clutter the composition.

The left side should contain the existing functional actions:

Play

Help & Credits

Play is the primary action and should use the green accent.

Help & Credits should be a restrained ghost/secondary action.

Do not change what either button does.

Play must continue to navigate to mode.

Help & Credits must continue to navigate to help.

Road composition

Use Seren’s #ribbon treatment as visual inspiration, not as something to copy literally.

Create an Alva-specific road element using HTML/CSS rather than adding a raster image.

The road should occupy approximately the right 40–48% of the Home composition on larger screens.

It should extend beyond the viewport vertically so it feels like a continuous piece of environment rather than a card.

Give it an angled/banked presentation, roughly in the neighborhood of 18–24 degrees.

Use charcoal asphalt tones such as:

linear-gradient(
  90deg,
  #111214,
  #242629 50%,
  #151618
)

Add subtle pale-gray lane markings.

The lane markings may move continuously to imply forward motion, similar to Seren’s Home road.

Keep the animation subtle.

Do not create a visually noisy racing effect.

The road should feel architectural and graphic.

Use soft dark fades at the top and bottom so the road dissolves naturally into the background.

Do not use Seren’s red/white kerbs.

This is Alva, not a racing-game clone.

Green slash separator

The key visual signature of this Home redesign should be a smooth diagonal green slash/line separating the large text area from the road.

The slash should follow or closely echo the left edge/angle of the road.

Use --accent for the primary line.

It should be crisp and anti-aliased through normal CSS transforms—not pixelated.

Aim for roughly 2–4 CSS pixels of visible thickness depending on resolution.

It can have an extremely restrained soft bloom such as:

box-shadow: 0 0 18px rgba(47,191,99,.20);

but do not turn it into a neon beam.

The green slash should be visually recognizable without overpowering the wordmark.

It should create the impression that the Home Screen is split into two zones:

ALVA / navigation / typography on the left

and

moving road / motion / game world on the right.

The separator should remain clean through responsive resizing.

Home actions

Keep the current two actions and their existing navigation.

Restyle them to fit the Seren-inspired language.

The Play button should be a green primary button with white text.

Keep its arrow/icon.

Use a relatively squared shape rather than a large soft pill. A small radius around 2–6px is appropriate.

Give the primary button restrained depth rather than a large shadow.

The secondary Help & Credits action should be transparent or lightly washed, with gray/off-white text.

Do not put both actions inside separate large floating cards.

The Home should feel open and typographic rather than card-heavy.

